# pengu: allow-warnings W0001
##
## std/functor.pengu — thick functions: a function pointer plus an explicit context.
##
## A `Fn0`/`Fn1`/`Fn2`/`Fn3` is the PenguScript answer to "a callback that
## remembers something" **without closures**. The value is a plain rune with two
## pointers: the function to call and the context to pass it. Construction is
## explicit and happens once; every call is one C indirect call.
##
## ## Why not closures
## PenguScript deliberately has no closures (see `LANGUAGE.md` §8.4). Zig, Odin
## and Nelua — the language's peers — do not have them either: a closure needs a
## garbage collector, lifetime tracking, or a non-portable trampoline, and all
## three would violate "explicit over hidden" and zero-cost abstractions. The
## idiom those languages use instead is a *thick function*: the caller passes
## the context explicitly, and the callee thread it through. This module is that
## idiom as a reusable type.
##
## ## Example
## ```pengu
## import std.functor
## import std.spark
##
## rune Multiplier:
##     factor as int
##
## weave apply with m as ref to Multiplier, x as int into int:
##     return m->factor * x
##
## weave main into int:
##     var m as Multiplier is with factor is 3
##     var f as functor.Fn1 of int, int is calling functor.make_fn1 with apply, (sigil of m)
##     calling spark.println with "{calling f.call with 5}"   # 15
##     return 0
## ```
##
## ## Memory
## Nothing here allocates: `make_fn*` returns a struct of two pointers and
## `call` is one indirect call. The context is **borrowed**, never owned: the
## caller must keep it alive for as long as the `Fn` is used. If the context
## owns memory, free it yourself (`defer banish ctx`) after the last call.
##
## ## Safety
## Construction reinterprets the function pointer with `transmute` so that its
## first parameter becomes `ref to void`. In strict C99, calling through an
## incompatible function-pointer type is technically undefined behaviour, but it
## is the standard Zig/Odin pattern and works on every real ABI (GCC, Clang,
## MSVC, TCC): a pointer is a pointer and the context is passed in the same
## register/stack slot either way. The reinterpretation is confined to
## `make_fn*`; `call` only calls the stored pointer with the stored context.
##
## ## See also
## `std.loom`'s `map_ctx`, `filter_ctx`, `fold_ctx`, `for_each_ctx`,
## `zip_with_ctx`, `find_first_ctx`, `any_ctx`, `all_ctx`, `partition_ctx` and
## `filter_map_ctx` — the pipeline functions that take the context explicitly
## and need no `Fn` at all. Use a `Fn` when the callback must be **stored** or
## **returned**; use `loom.*_ctx` when it is only called inside a loop.

import std.spark

insignia functor_

# --- Public constants ---------------------------------------------------

## The version of the functor module.
const FUNCTOR_VERSION as string is "2.9.0"

# --- Types --------------------------------------------------------------

## A thick function of no arguments: `fn_ptr(ctx) -> R`.
##
## `ctx` is the borrowed context; `fn_ptr` has been reinterpreted to receive it
## as `ref to void` (see `make_fn0`). Treat both fields as read-only.
rune Fn0 shard R:
    fn_ptr as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void into R
    ctx as ref to void

## A thick function of one argument: `fn_ptr(ctx, a) -> R`.
##
## `ctx` is the borrowed context; `fn_ptr` has been reinterpreted to receive it
## as `ref to void` (see `make_fn1`). Treat both fields as read-only.
rune Fn1 shard A, R:
    fn_ptr as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void, a as A into R
    ctx as ref to void

## A thick function of two arguments: `fn_ptr(ctx, a, b) -> R`.
##
## `ctx` is the borrowed context; `fn_ptr` has been reinterpreted to receive it
## as `ref to void` (see `make_fn2`). Treat both fields as read-only.
rune Fn2 shard A, B, R:
    fn_ptr as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void, a as A, b as B into R
    ctx as ref to void

## A thick function of three arguments: `fn_ptr(ctx, a, b, c) -> R`.
##
## `ctx` is the borrowed context; `fn_ptr` has been reinterpreted to receive it
## as `ref to void` (see `make_fn3`). Treat both fields as read-only.
rune Fn3 shard A, B, C, R:
    fn_ptr as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void, a as A, b as B, c as C into R
    ctx as ref to void

# --- Constructors -------------------------------------------------------

## Builds an `Fn0` from a function and a context.
##
## ## Parámetros
## - `f`: the function to store. Its first parameter is the typed context
##   (`c as ref to C`); any additional parameters become the call arguments.
## - `ctx`: the context passed to `f` on every call. It is **borrowed**: this
##   module never frees it.
##
## ## Retorno
## An `Fn0` (two pointers) that calls `f(ctx)`.
##
## ## Seguridad
## `f` is `transmute`d to the equivalent signature with `ref to void` as its
## first parameter. In strict C99, calling through an incompatible
## function-pointer type is technically undefined behaviour, but this is the
## standard Zig/Odin pattern and works on every real ABI. The reinterpretation
## happens here, once; `call` never casts.
##
## ## Tiempos de vida
## `ctx` must outlive the `Fn0` and every call made through it.
##
## ## Memoria
## No aloca memoria. The returned `Fn0` lives wherever the caller stores it.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## var counter as int is 0
## var f as functor.Fn0 of int is calling functor.make_fn0 with read_counter, (sigil of counter)
## calling f.call    # reads counter
## ```
weave make_fn0 shard R, C with f as weave with c as ref to C into R, ctx as ref to C into Fn0 of R:
    var fp as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void into R is transmute f to (ref to weave with ctx as ref to void into R)
    var cp as ref to void is transmute ctx to ref to void
    return with fn_ptr is fp, ctx is cp

## Builds an `Fn1` from a function and a context.
##
## ## Parámetros
## - `f`: the function to store. Its first parameter is the typed context
##   (`c as ref to C`); the second is the call argument.
## - `ctx`: the context passed to `f` on every call. It is **borrowed**: this
##   module never frees it.
##
## ## Retorno
## An `Fn1` (two pointers) that calls `f(ctx, a)`.
##
## ## Seguridad
## `f` is `transmute`d to the equivalent signature with `ref to void` as its
## first parameter. In strict C99, calling through an incompatible
## function-pointer type is technically undefined behaviour, but this is the
## standard Zig/Odin pattern and works on every real ABI. The reinterpretation
## happens here, once; `call` never casts.
##
## ## Tiempos de vida
## `ctx` must outlive the `Fn1` and every call made through it.
##
## ## Memoria
## No aloca memoria. The returned `Fn1` lives wherever the caller stores it.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## rune Multiplier:
##     factor as int
##
## weave apply with m as ref to Multiplier, x as int into int:
##     return m->factor * x
##
## var m as Multiplier is with factor is 3
## var f as functor.Fn1 of int, int is calling functor.make_fn1 with apply, (sigil of m)
## calling f.call with 5      # 15
## ```
weave make_fn1 shard A, R, C with f as weave with c as ref to C, a as A into R, ctx as ref to C into Fn1 of A, R:
    var fp as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void, a as A into R is transmute f to (ref to weave with ctx as ref to void, a as A into R)
    var cp as ref to void is transmute ctx to ref to void
    return with fn_ptr is fp, ctx is cp

## Builds an `Fn2` from a function and a context.
##
## ## Parámetros
## - `f`: the function to store. Its first parameter is the typed context
##   (`c as ref to C`); the next two are the call arguments.
## - `ctx`: the context passed to `f` on every call. It is **borrowed**: this
##   module never frees it.
##
## ## Retorno
## An `Fn2` (two pointers) that calls `f(ctx, a, b)`.
##
## ## Seguridad
## `f` is `transmute`d to the equivalent signature with `ref to void` as its
## first parameter. In strict C99, calling through an incompatible
## function-pointer type is technically undefined behaviour, but this is the
## standard Zig/Odin pattern and works on every real ABI. The reinterpretation
## happens here, once; `call` never casts.
##
## ## Tiempos de vida
## `ctx` must outlive the `Fn2` and every call made through it.
##
## ## Memoria
## No aloca memoria. The returned `Fn2` lives wherever the caller stores it.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## var f as functor.Fn2 of int, int, int is calling functor.make_fn2 with add, (sigil of base)
## calling f.call with 2, 3      # base + 5
## ```
weave make_fn2 shard A, B, R, C with f as weave with c as ref to C, a as A, b as B into R, ctx as ref to C into Fn2 of A, B, R:
    var fp as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void, a as A, b as B into R is transmute f to (ref to weave with ctx as ref to void, a as A, b as B into R)
    var cp as ref to void is transmute ctx to ref to void
    return with fn_ptr is fp, ctx is cp

## Builds an `Fn3` from a function and a context.
##
## The third argument type is named `C2` internally because `C` already names
## the context type (`ctx as ref to C`). This is a spelling detail of the
## declaration only; callers write `functor.Fn3 of A, B, C2, R` with their own
## names.
##
## ## Parámetros
## - `f`: the function to store. Its first parameter is the typed context
##   (`c as ref to C`); the next three are the call arguments.
## - `ctx`: the context passed to `f` on every call. It is **borrowed**: this
##   module never frees it.
##
## ## Retorno
## An `Fn3` (two pointers) that calls `f(ctx, a, b, c2)`.
##
## ## Seguridad
## `f` is `transmute`d to the equivalent signature with `ref to void` as its
## first parameter. In strict C99, calling through an incompatible
## function-pointer type is technically undefined behaviour, but this is the
## standard Zig/Odin pattern and works on every real ABI. The reinterpretation
## happens here, once; `call` never casts.
##
## ## Tiempos de vida
## `ctx` must outlive the `Fn3` and every call made through it.
##
## ## Memoria
## No aloca memoria. The returned `Fn3` lives wherever the caller stores it.
##
## ## Ejemplo
## ```pengu
## var f as functor.Fn3 of int, int, int, int is calling functor.make_fn3 with sum3, (sigil of base)
## calling f.call with 1, 2, 3
## ```
weave make_fn3 shard A, B, C2, R, C with f as weave with c as ref to C, a as A, b as B, c2 as C2 into R, ctx as ref to C into Fn3 of A, B, C2, R:
    var fp as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void, a as A, b as B, c2 as C2 into R is transmute f to (ref to weave with ctx as ref to void, a as A, b as B, c2 as C2 into R)
    var cp as ref to void is transmute ctx to ref to void
    return with fn_ptr is fp, ctx is cp

# --- Call surface -------------------------------------------------------

## Methods of `Fn0`: call it, ask whether it is null, or call it with a fallback.
enchanting Fn0 shard R:
    ## Calls the stored function with the stored context and returns its result.
    ##
    ## ## Retorno
    ## Whatever `fn_ptr` returns.
    ##
    ## ## Memoria
    ## No aloca memoria; the context is passed through unchanged.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling f.call
    ## ```
    weave call into R:
        var fp as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void into R is self->fn_ptr
        return calling fp with self->ctx

    ## Returns `true` when the `Fn0` holds no function.
    ##
    ## A `Fn0` built by `make_fn0` is never null (the function argument must be
    ## a real function); this is for values that crossed `donum` or were
    ## initialised field by field.
    weave is_null into bool:
        return self->fn_ptr == null

    ## Calls the stored function, or returns `fallback` when it is null.
    ##
    ## ## Memoria
    ## No aloca memoria. `fallback` is returned by value.
    weave call_or with fallback as R into R:
        if self->fn_ptr == null:
            return fallback
        var fp as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void into R is self->fn_ptr
        return calling fp with self->ctx

## Methods of `Fn1`: call it, ask whether it is null, or call it with a fallback.
enchanting Fn1 shard A, R:
    ## Calls the stored function with the stored context and `a`.
    ##
    ## ## Retorno
    ## Whatever `fn_ptr` returns.
    ##
    ## ## Memoria
    ## No aloca memoria; the context and `a` are passed through unchanged.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling f.call with 5
    ## ```
    weave call with a as A into R:
        var fp as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void, a as A into R is self->fn_ptr
        return calling fp with self->ctx, a

    ## Returns `true` when the `Fn1` holds no function.
    weave is_null into bool:
        return self->fn_ptr == null

    ## Calls the stored function with `a`, or returns `fallback` when null.
    ##
    ## ## Memoria
    ## No aloca memoria. `fallback` is returned by value.
    weave call_or with a as A, fallback as R into R:
        if self->fn_ptr == null:
            return fallback
        var fp as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void, a as A into R is self->fn_ptr
        return calling fp with self->ctx, a

## Methods of `Fn2`: call it, ask whether it is null, or call it with a fallback.
enchanting Fn2 shard A, B, R:
    ## Calls the stored function with the stored context, `a` and `b`.
    ##
    ## ## Memoria
    ## No aloca memoria; the context and the arguments pass through unchanged.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling f.call with 2, 3
    ## ```
    weave call with a as A, b as B into R:
        var fp as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void, a as A, b as B into R is self->fn_ptr
        return calling fp with self->ctx, a, b

    ## Returns `true` when the `Fn2` holds no function.
    weave is_null into bool:
        return self->fn_ptr == null

    ## Calls the stored function with `a` and `b`, or returns `fallback` when null.
    weave call_or with a as A, b as B, fallback as R into R:
        if self->fn_ptr == null:
            return fallback
        var fp as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void, a as A, b as B into R is self->fn_ptr
        return calling fp with self->ctx, a, b

## Methods of `Fn3`: call it, ask whether it is null, or call it with a fallback.
enchanting Fn3 shard A, B, C, R:
    ## Calls the stored function with the stored context, `a`, `b` and `c`.
    ##
    ## ## Memoria
    ## No aloca memoria; the context and the arguments pass through unchanged.
    ##
    ## ## Ejemplo
    ## ```pengu
    ## calling f.call with 1, 2, 3
    ## ```
    weave call with a as A, b as B, c as C into R:
        var fp as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void, a as A, b as B, c as C into R is self->fn_ptr
        return calling fp with self->ctx, a, b, c

    ## Returns `true` when the `Fn3` holds no function.
    weave is_null into bool:
        return self->fn_ptr == null

    ## Calls the stored function with the three arguments, or returns `fallback`.
    weave call_or with a as A, b as B, c as C, fallback as R into R:
        if self->fn_ptr == null:
            return fallback
        var fp as ref to weave with ctx as ref to void, a as A, b as B, c as C into R is self->fn_ptr
        return calling fp with self->ctx, a, b, c

# --- Tests --------------------------------------------------------------
# Private fixtures (see the module header). These run under `pengu test` and do
# not depend on `std.ward`: a panic is enough for a unit test.

## A three-times multiplier used by the constructor and call tests.
rune _Multiplier:
    factor as int

## A context that owns the expected text, for the `string` argument test.
rune _LengthCtx:
    expected as string

## A rune that stores a `Fn1`: the "callback as a field" case.
rune _Holder:
    f as Fn1 of int, int

## A mutable click counter for the `Fn1<A, void>` side-effect case.
rune _ClickCounter:
    hits as int

## Panics with `label` when `actual` and `expected` differ.
weave _expect_int with actual as int, expected as int, label as string into void:
    if actual != expected:
        calling spark.panic with "functor test failed: {label}: {actual} != {expected}"

## Panics with `label` when `cond` is false.
weave _expect with cond as bool, label as string into void:
    if not cond:
        calling spark.panic with "functor test failed: {label}"

## `(ref to _Multiplier, int) -> int`, the canonical context function.
weave _multiply with m as ref to _Multiplier, x as int into int:
    return m->factor * x

## `(ref to _Multiplier) -> int`, the zero-argument case.
weave _factor10 with m as ref to _Multiplier into int:
    return m->factor * 10

## `(ref to _Multiplier, int, int) -> int`, the two-argument case.
weave _multiply_sum with m as ref to _Multiplier, x as int, y as int into int:
    return m->factor * (x + y)

## `(ref to _Multiplier, int, int, int) -> int`, the three-argument case.
weave _multiply_sum3 with m as ref to _Multiplier, x as int, y as int, z as int into int:
    return m->factor * (x + y + z)

## `(ref to _Multiplier, ref to _Multiplier) -> int`, the `ref to rune` argument case.
weave _sum_factors with m as ref to _Multiplier, other as ref to _Multiplier into int:
    return m->factor + other->factor

## `(ref to _LengthCtx, string) -> bool`, the `string` argument case.
weave _string_len_is with c as ref to _LengthCtx, s as string into bool:
    return s == c->expected

## `(ref to int, int) -> int`, the plain-value context case.
weave _add_offset with offset as ref to int, x as int into int:
    return (essence of offset) + x

## `(ref to void, int) -> int`: ignores its context, for the null-context test.
weave _twice with c as ref to void, x as int into int:
    return x * 2

## `(ref to _ClickCounter, int) -> void`: the side-effect callback case.
weave _count_click with c as ref to _ClickCounter, button as int into void:
    set c->hits is c->hits + button

## Passes a `Fn1` to another function: the "callback as a parameter" case.
weave _run_it with f as (Fn1 of int, int), a as int into int:
    return calling f.call with a

# Zero-argument constructor and call.
test "make_fn0 calls the function with the context":
    var m as _Multiplier is with factor is 3
    var f as Fn0 of int is calling make_fn0 with _factor10, (sigil of m)
    calling _expect_int with (calling f.call), 30, "fn0"

# One-argument constructor and call, with a rune context.
test "make_fn1 calls the function with the context and the argument":
    var m as _Multiplier is with factor is 3
    var f as Fn1 of int, int is calling make_fn1 with _multiply, (sigil of m)
    calling _expect_int with (calling f.call with 5), 15, "fn1"

# A string argument.
test "make_fn1 carries a string argument":
    var c as _LengthCtx is with expected is "abc"
    var f as Fn1 of string, bool is calling make_fn1 with _string_len_is, (sigil of c)
    calling _expect with (calling f.call with "abc"), "fn1 string"
    calling _expect with not (calling f.call with "abcd"), "fn1 string mismatch"

# A `ref to rune` argument.
test "make_fn1 carries a reference argument":
    var a as _Multiplier is with factor is 2
    var b as _Multiplier is with factor is 5
    var f as Fn1 of ref to _Multiplier, int is calling make_fn1 with _sum_factors, (sigil of a)
    calling _expect_int with (calling f.call with (sigil of b)), 7, "fn1 ref"

# A plain-value context (ref to int rather than ref to rune).
test "make_fn1 accepts a plain-value context":
    var offset as int is 10
    var f as Fn1 of int, int is calling make_fn1 with _add_offset, (sigil of offset)
    calling _expect_int with (calling f.call with 4), 14, "fn1 int ctx"

# Two-argument constructor and call.
test "make_fn2 calls the function with two arguments":
    var m as _Multiplier is with factor is 3
    var f as Fn2 of int, int, int is calling make_fn2 with _multiply_sum, (sigil of m)
    calling _expect_int with (calling f.call with 2, 3), 15, "fn2"

# Three-argument constructor and call.
test "make_fn3 calls the function with three arguments":
    var m as _Multiplier is with factor is 2
    var f as Fn3 of int, int, int, int is calling make_fn3 with _multiply_sum3, (sigil of m)
    calling _expect_int with (calling f.call with 1, 2, 3), 12, "fn3"

# Storing a Fn1 in another rune.
test "a Fn1 can be stored in a rune field":
    var m as _Multiplier is with factor is 4
    var f as Fn1 of int, int is calling make_fn1 with _multiply, (sigil of m)
    var h as _Holder is with f is f
    calling _expect_int with (calling h.f.call with 3), 12, "fn1 in rune"

# Passing a Fn1 to another function.
test "a Fn1 can be passed to another function":
    var m as _Multiplier is with factor is 5
    var f as Fn1 of int, int is calling make_fn1 with _multiply, (sigil of m)
    calling _expect_int with (calling _run_it with f, 2), 10, "fn1 as parameter"

# A null context for a function that ignores it.
test "a Fn1 accepts a null context":
    var f as Fn1 of int, int is calling make_fn1 with _twice, null
    calling _expect with not (calling f.is_null), "null context not null"
    calling _expect_int with (calling f.call with 21), 42, "null context call"

# is_null and call_or on a real Fn.
test "is_null is false and call_or uses the function":
    var m as _Multiplier is with factor is 3
    var f as Fn1 of int, int is calling make_fn1 with _multiply, (sigil of m)
    calling _expect with not (calling f.is_null), "fn1 is_null"
    calling _expect_int with (calling f.call_or with 5, -1), 15, "fn1 call_or"

# is_null and call_or on a null Fn built field by field.
test "a donum Fn1 is null and call_or returns the fallback":
    var f as Fn1 of int, int is donum (Fn1 of int, int)
    calling _expect with (calling f.is_null), "donum is_null"
    calling _expect_int with (calling f.call_or with 5, -1), -1, "donum call_or"

# A `void` result: the callback exists for its side effect.
test "a Fn1 of void supports side effects":
    var c as _ClickCounter is with hits is 0
    var f as Fn1 of int, void is calling make_fn1 with _count_click, (sigil of c)
    calling f.call with 3
    calling f.call with 4
    calling _expect_int with c.hits, 7, "fn1 void"

# The version marker follows the toolchain release.
test "functor version is 2.9.0":
    calling _expect with FUNCTOR_VERSION == "2.9.0", "version"
